1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:08,736
Every photo, every word, every song, and every customer that a machine learning model sees becomes a list of numbers first.

2
00:00:09,162 --> 00:00:11,888
A photo becomes its pixel brightness values.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:14,986
A word becomes a few hundred learned numbers.

4
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:19,734
A customer becomes age, income, and number of purchases.

5
00:00:20,159 --> 00:00:21,702
That list of numbers has a name.

6
00:00:22,114 --> 00:00:23,031
It is called a vector.

7
00:00:23,973 --> 00:00:29,571
In this video you will learn the two moves that vectors allow: adding and scaling.

8
00:00:30,023 --> 00:00:31,433
They look simple, and they are.

9
00:00:31,872 --> 00:00:39,944
But every other idea in this series, from matrices to gradients to attention, is built from these two moves.

10
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:42,550
You only need arithmetic to follow.

11
00:00:42,976 --> 00:00:44,744
Let's discover it step by step.

12
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:50,555
A vector has three faces, and it helps to know all three.

13
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:52,749
The first face is an arrow.

14
00:00:53,188 --> 00:00:53,946
Here is a grid.

15
00:00:54,358 --> 00:00:56,406
Start at the center, the origin.

16
00:00:56,805 --> 00:00:59,558
Walk three steps to the right and two steps up.

17
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:03,082
The arrow from the origin to that point is a vector.

18
00:01:03,989 --> 00:01:05,757
The second face is a list.

19
00:01:06,183 --> 00:01:10,318
We can write the same arrow as two numbers: three and two.

20
00:01:10,744 --> 00:01:12,512
Each number is called a component.

21
00:01:12,951 --> 00:01:15,797
The first component says how far to go right.

22
00:01:16,223 --> 00:01:19,055
The second component says how far to go up.

23
00:01:19,494 --> 00:01:23,111
The arrow and the list carry exactly the same information.

24
00:01:24,632 --> 00:01:26,759
Lists are how data looks in practice.

25
00:01:27,171 --> 00:01:27,956
Take a house.

26
00:01:28,395 --> 00:01:33,568
It has one hundred twenty square meters, three bedrooms, and it is fifteen years old.

27
00:01:34,007 --> 00:01:36,187
That is a vector with three components.

28
00:01:36,626 --> 00:01:44,352
We cannot draw three-dimensional houses as flat arrows easily, and with a hundred features we cannot draw them at all.

29
00:01:44,778 --> 00:01:46,626
But the list still works perfectly.

30
00:01:48,132 --> 00:01:50,379
The third face is the deepest one.

31
00:01:50,791 --> 00:02:00,459
Mathematicians say: a vector is anything you can add to another thing of its kind, and stretch by a number, and still get the same kind of thing back.

32
00:02:00,871 --> 00:02:01,802
Arrows do that.

33
00:02:02,227 --> 00:02:03,012
Lists do that.

34
00:02:03,424 --> 00:02:06,257
Later we will see that even sound waves do that.

35
00:02:06,695 --> 00:02:09,781
Keep these two moves in mind: add and scale.

36
00:02:11,439 --> 00:02:12,357
Let's start with adding.

37
00:02:12,809 --> 00:02:16,692
Here is a blue vector u, equal to three, one.

38
00:02:17,131 --> 00:02:20,216
Here is a green vector v, equal to one, two.

39
00:02:20,655 --> 00:02:21,878
What should u plus v mean?

40
00:02:22,357 --> 00:02:23,740
Think of it as two walks.

41
00:02:24,218 --> 00:02:25,322
First walk along u.

42
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:28,540
Then, from where you stopped, walk along v.

43
00:02:29,473 --> 00:02:31,441
Put the tail of v on the tip of u.

44
00:02:31,879 --> 00:02:35,802
The yellow arrow from the origin to the final point is u plus v.

45
00:02:36,241 --> 00:02:36,933
Where did we end up?

46
00:02:37,438 --> 00:02:39,260
Three plus one is four steps right.

47
00:02:39,699 --> 00:02:42,212
One plus two is three steps up.

48
00:02:42,651 --> 00:02:44,805
So u plus v equals four, three.

49
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:46,516
Notice the rule.

50
00:02:46,955 --> 00:02:50,785
To add two vectors, you add them component by component.

51
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:53,604
First with first, second with second.

52
00:02:54,003 --> 00:02:54,601
Nothing more.

53
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:57,966
The arrow picture and the list rule always agree.

54
00:02:58,391 --> 00:03:04,242
That is why we can trust the list rule even in a hundred dimensions, where no picture exists.

55
00:03:05,178 --> 00:03:05,976
Does the order matter?

56
00:03:06,402 --> 00:03:07,838
Walk v first, then u.

57
00:03:08,277 --> 00:03:13,410
You trace the other side of this shape, a parallelogram, and you arrive at the same point.

58
00:03:13,835 --> 00:03:16,721
So u plus v equals v plus u.

59
00:03:17,146 --> 00:03:21,561
This is our first rule of vectors: the order of adding does not matter.

60
00:03:23,273 --> 00:03:25,201
Now the second move: scaling.

61
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,193
We multiply a vector by an ordinary number.

62
00:03:28,605 --> 00:03:34,270
In linear algebra, an ordinary single number is called a scalar, because it scales things.

63
00:03:34,709 --> 00:03:36,663
Take u equal to two, one.

64
00:03:37,102 --> 00:03:38,618
Two times u is four, two.

65
00:03:39,044 --> 00:03:42,235
The arrow keeps its direction and gets twice as long.

66
00:03:43,175 --> 00:03:44,904
Watch the scalar c change.

67
00:03:45,343 --> 00:03:48,468
When c is bigger than one, the arrow stretches.

68
00:03:48,893 --> 00:03:52,444
When c is between zero and one, it shrinks.

69
00:03:52,856 --> 00:03:56,686
At zero, it collapses to the zero vector, a point.

70
00:03:57,085 --> 00:04:02,138
And when c is negative, the arrow flips around and points the opposite way.

71
00:04:03,049 --> 00:04:05,469
The rule is again component by component.

72
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:08,408
Multiply every component by the scalar.

73
00:04:08,847 --> 00:04:12,544
Minus one times two, one is minus two, minus one.

74
00:04:12,996 --> 00:04:16,134
One half times two, one is one, one half.

75
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:21,134
Every multiple of u lies on the same straight line through the origin.

76
00:04:22,859 --> 00:04:24,455
Now combine the two moves.

77
00:04:24,881 --> 00:04:26,795
Scale some vectors, then add them.

78
00:04:27,234 --> 00:04:29,734
The result is called a linear combination.

79
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:38,790
With two vectors u and v, a linear combination looks like a times u plus b times v, where a and b are scalars.

80
00:04:39,216 --> 00:04:42,420
This single pattern is the heart of linear algebra.

81
00:04:43,352 --> 00:04:44,044
Here is a puzzle.

82
00:04:44,483 --> 00:04:47,741
Let u be two, one, and v be one, two.

83
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:50,108
Can we reach the yellow point five, four?

84
00:04:50,547 --> 00:04:52,993
Try a equals two and b equals one.

85
00:04:53,419 --> 00:04:55,015
Two times u is four, two.

86
00:04:55,427 --> 00:04:57,661
Add v, which is one, two.

87
00:04:58,100 --> 00:04:59,217
Four plus one is five.

88
00:04:59,642 --> 00:05:01,398
Two plus two is four.

89
00:05:01,823 --> 00:05:03,472
Yes, we reached five, four.

90
00:05:04,393 --> 00:05:08,874
In fact, with these two vectors we can reach every point on the whole plane.

91
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:10,988
Just choose the right a and b.

92
00:05:11,401 --> 00:05:15,137
All the points you can reach with linear combinations are called the span.

93
00:05:15,589 --> 00:05:18,927
Here, the span of u and v is the entire plane.

94
00:05:20,456 --> 00:05:21,280
But be careful.

95
00:05:21,706 --> 00:05:28,408
If the two vectors point along the same line, like two, one and four, two, the span is only that line.

96
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:31,533
The second vector adds nothing new.

97
00:05:31,972 --> 00:05:39,671
This idea of redundant vectors is so important that it gets its own video: basis and rank, video three.

98
00:05:41,406 --> 00:05:42,549
So what is a vector, really?

99
00:05:43,002 --> 00:05:44,757
Here is the precise answer.

100
00:05:45,156 --> 00:05:49,212
A vector space is a collection of objects where two things are true.

101
00:05:49,677 --> 00:05:52,616
First, adding two members gives another member.

102
00:05:53,042 --> 00:05:56,087
Second, scaling a member gives another member.

103
00:05:56,526 --> 00:05:58,081
You never fall out of the collection.

104
00:05:59,001 --> 00:06:03,522
The adding and scaling must also follow a few common-sense rules.

105
00:06:03,935 --> 00:06:07,764
Order does not matter: u plus v equals v plus u.

106
00:06:08,216 --> 00:06:10,703
There is a zero vector that changes nothing.

107
00:06:11,102 --> 00:06:13,948
Every vector has an opposite that cancels it.

108
00:06:14,387 --> 00:06:20,703
And scaling spreads over a sum: a times u plus v equals a u plus a v.

109
00:06:22,248 --> 00:06:23,764
Here is the surprise.

110
00:06:24,269 --> 00:06:25,931
Functions follow these rules too.

111
00:06:26,357 --> 00:06:27,713
Take two sound waves.

112
00:06:28,112 --> 00:06:31,490
Add them, point by point, and you get a new sound wave.

113
00:06:31,902 --> 00:06:36,317
Make one louder by multiplying it by two, and it is still a sound wave.

114
00:06:36,742 --> 00:06:40,200
So sound waves are vectors, even though they are not arrows.

115
00:06:41,734 --> 00:06:44,474
That is why the definition is so powerful.

116
00:06:44,886 --> 00:06:52,479
Anything we prove using only add and scale works for arrows, for lists of data, for sound, and for images.

117
00:06:52,891 --> 00:06:58,304
In this series, this is our first axiom, axiom A: linearity.

118
00:06:58,716 --> 00:07:00,724
Everything else is built on top of it.

119
00:07:02,439 --> 00:07:04,899
Now let's see vectors at work in machine learning.

120
00:07:05,338 --> 00:07:09,274
Here are two tiny toy images, just two by two pixels.

121
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:12,878
Each pixel is a brightness between zero and one.

122
00:07:13,317 --> 00:07:19,261
Write the four pixels in a row, and each image becomes a vector with four components.

123
00:07:20,173 --> 00:07:23,657
What is one half of the first image plus one half of the second?

124
00:07:24,082 --> 00:07:25,478
It is a linear combination.

125
00:07:25,904 --> 00:07:28,856
Each pixel becomes the average of the two pixels.

126
00:07:29,295 --> 00:07:32,008
The result is a blend of the two pictures.

127
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:38,856
Mixing images, mixing sounds, averaging data: all of them are linear combinations.

128
00:07:40,373 --> 00:07:42,420
And here is the most important example.

129
00:07:42,806 --> 00:07:50,585
A neuron in a neural network takes an input vector, multiplies each component by a weight, and adds everything up.

130
00:07:51,024 --> 00:07:53,923
That weighted sum is a linear combination too.

131
00:07:54,362 --> 00:07:59,907
Billions of these sums, stacked together, are what a large language model computes.

132
00:08:01,606 --> 00:08:02,470
Now it is your turn.

133
00:08:02,909 --> 00:08:04,704
Pause the video and try this.

134
00:08:05,143 --> 00:08:09,917
What is three times the vector one, minus two, plus the vector two, five?

135
00:08:10,343 --> 00:08:15,821
Use the two rules: scale component by component, then add component by component.

136
00:08:16,247 --> 00:08:17,510
Take a few seconds.

137
00:08:18,458 --> 00:08:19,721
Here is the answer.

138
00:08:20,213 --> 00:08:24,282
Three times one, minus two, is three, minus six.

139
00:08:24,721 --> 00:08:26,011
Now add two, five.

140
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:28,617
Three plus two is five.

141
00:08:29,056 --> 00:08:31,503
Minus six plus five is minus one.

142
00:08:31,942 --> 00:08:34,216
So the answer is five, minus one.

143
00:08:34,641 --> 00:08:38,405
If you got it, you already know how to compute any linear combination.

144
00:08:39,931 --> 00:08:41,726
One more question, about span.

145
00:08:42,165 --> 00:08:44,173
Use only one vector, two, one.

146
00:08:44,572 --> 00:08:46,527
Can you reach the point six, three?

147
00:08:46,953 --> 00:08:49,359
And can you reach the point one, one?

148
00:08:49,785 --> 00:08:55,862
Remember, with one vector, a linear combination is just c times that vector.

149
00:08:56,288 --> 00:08:57,165
Pause and think.

150
00:08:58,065 --> 00:09:02,253
Six, three is three times two, one, so yes, it is in the span.

151
00:09:02,692 --> 00:09:05,631
But one, one is not a multiple of two, one.

152
00:09:06,070 --> 00:09:09,288
To get the first component one, c must be one half.

153
00:09:09,714 --> 00:09:12,745
Then the second component is one half, not one.

154
00:09:13,184 --> 00:09:18,277
So one, one is outside the span: one vector only reaches a line.

155
00:09:19,959 --> 00:09:25,677
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy, a library for working with arrays of numbers.

156
00:09:26,090 --> 00:09:33,191
We make u and v, then add them, scale them, and build the linear combination two u plus v.

157
00:09:33,616 --> 00:09:38,576
Then we ask NumPy to find a and b for the point five, four.

158
00:09:39,055 --> 00:09:41,661
Finally, we blend two toy images.

159
00:09:43,182 --> 00:09:46,985
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

160
00:09:47,411 --> 00:09:49,033
u plus v is four, three.

161
00:09:49,459 --> 00:09:51,028
Two times u is four, two.

162
00:09:51,467 --> 00:09:59,365
Two u plus v is five, four, and NumPy found a equals two and b equals one, just like our puzzle.

163
00:09:59,778 --> 00:10:02,929
The blended image is the pixel-by-pixel average.

164
00:10:04,639 --> 00:10:05,278
In summary.

165
00:10:05,676 --> 00:10:08,482
A vector is anything you can add and scale.

166
00:10:08,881 --> 00:10:14,440
In practice, it is a list of numbers, and in two dimensions you can draw it as an arrow.

167
00:10:14,865 --> 00:10:17,272
You add and scale component by component.

168
00:10:17,698 --> 00:10:21,142
Scale and then add, and you get a linear combination.

169
00:10:21,567 --> 00:10:23,815
All the reachable points form the span.

170
00:10:24,746 --> 00:10:31,568
In machine learning, every data point is a vector, and every neuron computes a linear combination.

171
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:41,009
Next, in video two, we stack many vectors side by side and get a matrix: a grid of numbers that is also a machine that moves vectors.

172
00:10:41,448 --> 00:10:42,007
See you there.

173
00:10:43,873 --> 00:10:45,694
This video was made by David R.

174
00:10:46,133 --> 00:10:49,365
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

175
00:10:49,923 --> 00:10:50,641
Thanks for watching.
